From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 17:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: put the display engine behind IOMMU1

Name IOMMU1 master 2 in `iommus` on the mixer and on the display-engine
(KMS) node. The engine then fetches through IOVAs, and any page of RAM --
also above 4 GiB -- is reachable through its 32-bit layer address register
without swiotlb.

  master 2    DE0 is master 2 of IOMMU1 (A733 UM V0.92, chapter 10).
              On the 8 GiB board on 2026-09-15 the vendor system ran
              the display engine translating with IOMMU1 master 2's BYP at
              0, and its layer and RCQ addresses walked through the page
              table to buffers that equal the DE registers word for word.

⚠ ON BOTH NODES, THE SAME MASTER. The GEM DMA helpers allocate against the
KMS device and a PRIME import maps through it (drm_dev_dma_dev() returns
drm->dev unless a driver sets another), while the mixer fetches -- and the mixer's
own sun60i-de framebuffer is allocated against the mixer. Both have to live
in one address space. Naming the same IOMMU in both places puts both devices
into that IOMMU's one group (the driver uses
generic_single_device_group()), so they share one default domain and one
table. sun60i-kms.c already refuses to bind when only one of the two is
behind an IOMMU.
Mainline's sun8i-mixer gets there differently: it calls of_dma_configure()
on the DRM device from the mixer's bind. 6.18 answers that with
"late IOMMU probe at driver bind, something fishy here!" and a WARNING --
seen on a second board whose vendor-derived DRM driver does exactly that. Describing it in the DT
lets the core configure both devices before either binds.

The comments that said this tree has no IOMMU are corrected where they
stand: the mixer node's, sun60i-de.c's above sun60i_de_layer() ("OUR TREE HAS
NO IOMMU SUPPORT AT ALL", "MUST NOT BE ADDED SPECULATIVELY") and
sun60i-kms.c's above sun60i_kms_check_dma_domain() ("they would not arrive
for this node"). The omission was right while no IOMMU driver existed
(scanout from the physical 0xfe400000 on 2026-08-19); it is what capped
scanout at 4 GiB. Comment-only in the two C files.

VERIFIED HERE: applies with fuzz 0 on top of the full series; the dtb built
with cpp + dtc carries iommus = <&iommu1 2> on /display-engine and on
/soc/mixer@5100000 (phandle 0x1c = iommu@3910000).

HARDWARE-PROVEN on the 8 GiB board 2026-09-18, test kernel beside the
card's own kernel, same rootfs in both arms:
  * iommu@3910000 logs "translating: L1 table at 0x0000000101e84000,
    TTB 0x407a1" and "master 2 translating"; /sys/kernel/iommu_groups holds
    display-engine and 5100000.mixer in group 0; IOMMU1 m2 BYP (0x03910804)
    reads 0 and every other master 1; no fault line from either node; DP-1
    stays connected at 1920x1200 with a picture.
  * the VI0 layer address 0x05101018 reads 0xFD000000 -- an IOVA, against
    the physical 0xF0C00000 the control kernel reads -- and walks from
    IOMMU1's TTB through DTE 0x21549101 and PTE 0x0548910E to page
    0x105489000, R and W set, above 4 GiB. The table scan: 9 valid L1
    entries, 6750 mapped pages, all at or above 4 GiB.
  * glmark2-es2-wayland for 60 s in a labwc session: zero swiotlb_bounced
    events for display-engine and no "swiotlb buffer is full" in the whole
    boot, against 294 events and four full-buffer lines on the same image
    with the default kernel.

CORRECTED HERE: an earlier version of this header named 'sun60i-de logs
"scanout buffer at ... -- an IOVA"' as a falsifier. That line cannot appear in
a KMS boot: it sits in sun60i_de_enable(), which is the debugfs bring-up path
and owns its own framebuffer, while the KMS path allocates through the GEM DMA
helpers and leaves de->fb NULL (sun60i-de.c, the comment above
sun60i_de_enable()). The register readback above is the evidence instead, and
it is the better one -- the kernel is the thing under test.

NO PERFORMANCE CLAIM. glmark2 scored 72 on the default kernel and 63 in the
measured run; three further runs on the same kernel and the same boot scored
53, 50 and 77. The
spread swallows the difference, governor and thermal state were uncontrolled,
and the two arms do not render the same path -- the default kernel composites
once swiotlb refuses a mapping.

Assisted-by: Claude
---

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -665,10 +665,23 @@ ramoops@60000000 {
 	 * two display outputs and could drive a second pipe,
 	 * but only disp0 -> TCON_TV1 -> DisplayPort has ever been on a board.
 	 * A second entry here would describe a pipeline nothing has proven.
+	 *
+	 * `iommus` HERE AS WELL AS ON THE MIXER, and it is the same master.
+	 * This node's device is the one the GEM DMA helpers allocate against
+	 * and the one a PRIME import maps through (drm_dev_dma_dev() returns
+	 * drm->dev unless a driver sets another); the mixer is the block that
+	 * fetches. Both have to see one address space, so both name IOMMU1
+	 * master 2, and the IOMMU driver puts every device of one IOMMU into
+	 * one group with one page table. sun60i-kms.c refuses to bind if only
+	 * one of the two is behind an IOMMU. Mainline's sun8i-mixer reaches
+	 * the same result by calling of_dma_configure() on the DRM device from
+	 * the mixer's bind, which 6.18 answers with "late IOMMU probe at
+	 * driver bind".
 	 */
 	display-engine {
 		compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-display-engine";
 		allwinner,pipelines = <&mixer0>;
+		iommus = <&iommu1 2>;
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 
@@ -1580,22 +1593,22 @@ ohci1: usb@4200400 {
 		 *                  driver DOES power-gate this domain it must then
 		 *                  treat the per-channel scaler coefficient SRAM as
 		 *                  uninitialised.
-		 *   iommus         ✅ VINDICATED ON SILICON, 2026-08-19. The stated
-		 *                  falsifier was "a scanout that fetches nothing from a
-		 *                  physically-addressed buffer". It fetched: the engine
-		 *                  scanned 1920x1200 from the PHYSICAL address
-		 *                  0xfe400000 with no IOMMU bound, onto a monitor. So
-		 *                  the omission is now correct by measurement rather
-		 *                  than by caution. ⚠ And the vendor's own capture is
-		 *                  NOT comparable on this point -- it runs the same path
-		 *                  through sunxi-iommu-v2 as master 8, so its layer
-		 *                  address 0xfc000000 is an IOVA, not a physical
-		 *                  address. Whoever one day adds an IOMMU driver adds
-		 *                  the property then.
+		 *
 		 *   DE_SYS_BGR, the AHB matrix gate, the MBUS matrix gate: all three
 		 *                  are in their working state at power-on and the
 		 *                  vendor writes none of them -- the CCU patch lists
 		 *                  them with their reset values.
+		 *
+		 * `iommus` IS HERE NOW, and what it replaces was measured, not
+		 * assumed: on 2026-08-19 this engine scanned 1920x1200 from the
+		 * PHYSICAL address 0xfe400000 with no IOMMU bound -- its IOMMU1
+		 * master resets in bypass. That scanout is limited to what a
+		 * 32-bit layer address reaches, and a GPU buffer above 4 GiB was
+		 * bounced through swiotlb or failed to import.
+		 * Master 2 of IOMMU1 is DE0; the
+		 * vendor's cell 8 is the same master in its one-node numbering
+		 * (6 masters per IOMMU, 6 + 2). The display-engine node above
+		 * names the same master, and says why.
 		 */
 		mixer0: mixer@5100000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-de-mixer-0";
@@ -1610,6 +1623,7 @@ mixer0: mixer@5100000 {
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_DE0>;
 			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_DE0>;
 			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_DE_PLL3X>;
+			iommus = <&iommu1 2>;
 			status = "okay";
 
 			/*
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-de.c
@@ -1145,16 +1145,16 @@ static void sun60i_de_blender_window(struct sun60i_de *de)
  *     cannot, because 0xFC000000 there is an IOVA and whatever this driver
  *     programs here is not. Do not "fix" a mismatch in this register against
  *     the capture.
- *  2. OUR TREE HAS NO IOMMU SUPPORT AT ALL -- no patch mentions one, no DT node
- *     exists, no `iommus` property is anywhere in it -- so on our kernel the
- *     DE's address reaches the bus UNTRANSLATED. That is correct if and only if
- *     the IOMMU is genuinely in bypass or off for us, and NOTHING HAS VERIFIED
- *     THAT. If it comes up translating with no mapping for master 8, the DE
- *     fetches from nowhere and the symptom is a black screen with every
- *     register reading back perfectly -- the exact failure shape that cost this
- *     project a session on 2026-08-19. So the driver does not assume it: it
- *     asks device_iommu_mapped() and SAYS which of the two it got, at enable
- *     and in `state`.
+ *  2. WHETHER OUR ADDRESS IS AN IOVA DEPENDS ON THE DEVICE TREE. Without an
+ *     `iommus` property the DE's address reaches the bus untranslated -- its
+ *     IOMMU1 master resets in bypass, and on 2026-08-19 it was seen fetching from the
+ *     physical 0xfe400000. With `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1`'s `iommus = <&iommu1 2>` (IOMMU driver
+ *     `iommu-sun60i-add-the-allwinner-a733-iommu-driver`) it is an IOVA. A mismatch -- translating with no mapping for
+ *     master 2 -- fetches from nowhere, and the symptom is a black screen with
+ *     every register reading back perfectly, the failure shape that cost this
+ *     project a session on 2026-08-19. So the driver does not assume either:
+ *     it asks device_iommu_mapped() and SAYS which of the two it got, at
+ *     enable and in `state`.
  *
  * WHY THE DMA API RATHER THAN A HAND-ROLLED ADDRESS, and it is this exact case:
  * dma_alloc_coherent() hands back a dma_addr_t, which is a physical address
@@ -1162,11 +1162,12 @@ static void sun60i_de_blender_window(struct sun60i_de *de)
  * `iommus` property exist -- with no change in this file. A hand-computed
  * physical address would be silently wrong on that day.
  *
- * ⛔ AND THE `iommus` PROPERTY MUST NOT BE ADDED SPECULATIVELY. A phandle to an
- * IOMMU node with no driver bound leaves this device deferring forever, so it
- * arrives with a verified IOMMU driver or not at all. Mainline 6.18.36 does
- * ship drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c; whether it matches this SoC's
- * sunxi-iommu-v2 is UNVERIFIED and this file assumes neither way.
+ * ⛔ THE `iommus` PROPERTY ARRIVES ONLY WITH ITS DRIVER. A phandle to an IOMMU
+ * node with no driver bound leaves this device deferring forever. Mainline's
+ * sun50i-iommu.c is the H6 layout and does not fit this SoC
+ * -- the driver is `iommu-sun60i-add-the-allwinner-a733-iommu-driver`'s sun60i-iommu.c, and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1` adds the
+ * property together with it. The vendor's master id 8 is IOMMU1 master 2 in
+ * its one-node numbering.
  */
 static void sun60i_de_layer(struct sun60i_de *de)
 {
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-kms.c
@@ -1039,11 +1039,14 @@ static int sun60i_kms_topology(struct device *dev,
  * two agree; that is what `drm-a733-display-engine-dram-into-tcon-tv1` measured on 2026-08-19, scanning 1920x1200 from
  * the physical 0xfe400000.
  *
- * The day an IOMMU driver and an `iommus` property arrive for the engine, they
- * would not arrive for this node, and the address handed to the layer register
- * would be a physical address where the engine expects an IOVA. The screen
- * would go black with every register reading back correct -- the failure shape
- * this pipe has already paid for twice. So the two are compared, once, at bind.
+ * With an IOMMU in the path (`iommu-sun60i-add-the-allwinner-a733-iommu-driver`, and `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1`'s `iommus = <&iommu1 2>` on the
+ * engine) both must be translated through the same table. `arm64-dts-a733-display-engine-behind-iommu1` therefore names
+ * the same IOMMU master on this node too, and the IOMMU driver puts both
+ * devices into one group with one domain. A device tree that gives the property
+ * to only one of the two would hand the layer register a physical address where
+ * the engine expects an IOVA, or the reverse: a black screen with every register
+ * reading back correct -- the failure shape this pipe has already paid for
+ * twice. So the two are compared, once, at bind.
  */
 static int sun60i_kms_check_dma_domain(struct device *master, struct device *de)
 {
